# slatwall-ts — ignore rules for the TypeScript subtree
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT OPTIONAL
#
# The repository root already carries a `.gitignore`, but it predates this
# subtree and is one of the untouched CFML-era files this port must not modify
# (AAP 0.9.5: "git status shows only additions under slatwall-ts/"; AAP 0.4.1:
# "Zero existing repository files are UPDATEd"). It ignores none of the four
# artefact classes a Node/TypeScript build produces — not `node_modules/`, not
# `dist/`, not coverage output, and not `.env`.
#
# Two mandated gates depend on the rules below:
#
#   * AAP 0.9.5 — "Zero existing repository files are modified. `git status`
#     shows only additions under `slatwall-ts/`." Without these rules the
#     installed dependency tree (tens of thousands of files), the emitted Lambda
#     bundle and the coverage report all appear as untracked additions, and the
#     gate cannot be evaluated, let alone pass.
#
#   * AAP 0.8.3 / 0.9.5 — "Environment-driven configuration with no hardcoded
#     credentials, accompanied by a committed `.env.example` documenting the
#     contract" and "No credential is hardcoded." `slatwall-ts/.env.example` is
#     the committed contract and carries no secret; a developer's real `.env`
#     carries `DB_PASSWORD` and must never become committable. The `.env`
#     /`.env.*` pair with the `!.env.example` re-inclusion is what keeps the
#     contract tracked and the secret untracked.
#
# The rules are deliberately scoped to this subtree. Nothing here can affect the
# CFML tree, because a `.gitignore` applies to its own directory and below.

# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build output — `npm run bundle` / `npm run package` write here (esbuild.config.mjs)
dist/
build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Test / coverage output
coverage/

# Tooling caches
.eslintcache
.vitest/

# Local environment (the committed contract is .env.example)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
